  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Guides",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Acrylic vs Penetrating Concrete Sealer",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/acrylic-vs-penetrating-concrete-sealer/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is penetrating sealer better than acrylic?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For exterior concrete that faces freeze-thaw, de-icing salt or vehicle traffic, yes — it protects inside the pore structure where surface wear cannot reach it, and lasts five to ten years against two to four. For decorative concrete where colour and sheen are the objective, acrylic is the right product and penetrating sealer does nothing at all visually."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you put acrylic sealer over penetrating sealer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually not reliably. The penetrating sealer has made the pore walls water-repellent, and a water-based acrylic will not bond to that surface. Solvent-based acrylics sometimes adhere but often go on patchy. Decide on appearance before you apply any penetrating product, because the decision is effectively permanent."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which sealer is best for a concrete driveway?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "In a climate with winter salting and freeze-thaw cycles, a silane-siloxane penetrating sealer. It survives plough and tyre wear because there is no surface film to remove, and it will not peel or blister. In a mild climate where appearance matters more than durability, a solvent acrylic is a reasonable alternative."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does penetrating sealer change the look of concrete?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Applied correctly, no. It should be invisible once cured. If a penetrating sealer has darkened the slab or left a sheen, it was applied too heavily and excess product dried on the surface — which can usually be corrected by scrubbing with the matching solvent while it is fresh."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will acrylic sealer peel?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It can, and that is its main failure mode. Peeling happens when the film is applied over a dirty or damp slab, applied too thick, or subjected to vapour pushing up from beneath. A well-applied acrylic on a dry, clean, well-drained slab wears away gradually rather than peeling, but it still needs recoating every two to four years."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which lasts longer, acrylic or penetrating sealer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Penetrating, by a factor of two to four. A silane-siloxane gives five to ten years on a residential driveway; a water-based acrylic gives one to three and a solvent acrylic two to four. The gap widens further in salted, high-traffic conditions where film wear is fastest."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use penetrating sealer on a stamped patio?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "You can, and it will protect the concrete, but it will do nothing for the colour and sheen that stamping is usually done for. Stamped work is one of the few cases where the decorative film is the point, so an acrylic — with a slip additive — is the right choice despite its shorter life."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
