    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#mechanism" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How Each One Works</a>
          <a href="#appearance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Appearance and Sheen</a>
          <a href="#failure" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. How Each Fails</a>
          <a href="#freeze" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Freeze-Thaw and Salt</a>
          <a href="#slip" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Slip, Heat and Tyres</a>
          <a href="#surfaces" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Matching Sealer to Surface</a>
          <a href="#switching" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Switching Between Them</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The question is not which sealer is better. It is whether you are protecting concrete or decorating it, because no product on the shelf does both well.
        </p>

        <div id="mechanism" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Each One Actually Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            An acrylic sealer is a film. The carrier evaporates and leaves a thin plastic layer bonded to the top of the slab, typically a few thousandths of an inch thick. Water, oil and salt are kept out because they never reach the concrete — they sit on the film. Everything good and bad about acrylics follows from that one fact.
          </p>

          <p>
            A penetrating sealer is a chemical reaction inside the concrete. Silanes and siloxanes are small enough to travel into the capillary pores, where they react with moisture and free lime to form a water-repellent lining on the pore walls. The pores stay open to vapour but stop drawing in liquid water. Nothing sits on the surface, which is why a properly applied penetrating sealer is invisible.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE ONE-LINE VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Acrylic protects the surface. Penetrating sealer protects the concrete.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A film keeps contaminants off the top face and wears away under traffic and UV. A penetrating sealer lines the pore structure a quarter inch down, where nothing can scrub it off. That is the whole comparison, and every other difference follows from it.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Within penetrating sealers, the distinctions between silane, siloxane, blends, silicates and siliconates matter more than most labels admit — molecule size determines how deeply a product travels and what substrate it suits. If you are choosing between specific penetrating products, our guide to <a href='/silane-vs-siloxane-sealers/'>silane versus siloxane sealers</a> unpicks the terminology properly.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a hybrid category worth knowing about: siliconate and silicate products that harden the surface as well as repel water, and blended systems that put a thin acrylic over a penetrating base. They are legitimate, but they are compromises, and they usually inherit the acrylic's maintenance schedule rather than the penetrating sealer's.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="appearance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Appearance, Sheen and Colour
          </h2>

          <p>
            Acrylics darken concrete and add sheen, ranging from a barely perceptible satin to a high gloss wet look. On stamped, coloured or exposed aggregate concrete, that enhancement is frequently the entire reason for sealing — the decorative work only reads properly when the colour is deepened. Penetrating sealers, applied correctly, change nothing about how the slab looks.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Property</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Acrylic (Film-Forming)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Penetrating (Silane-Siloxane)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Appearance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Darkens, adds sheen</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No visible change</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wet look available</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Can be tinted</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Shows application errors</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes — laps, streaks, roller marks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rarely</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">UV yellowing risk</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Solvent acrylics can amber</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Reversible</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strippable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Permanent</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            That last row deserves attention. A penetrating sealer cannot be removed once it is in the concrete. If you seal a patio with a silane-siloxane and later decide you want the wet look, you have a problem: acrylic will not bond to a water-repellent surface, and the repellency does not wash off. Sealing decoratively later is a decision you have to make before you seal at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            Acrylics also show workmanship. Lap marks, roller texture, streaking and cloudiness are all film defects and all visible. A penetrating sealer applied slightly unevenly produces no visual evidence whatsoever, which makes it considerably more forgiving for a first-time DIY application on a large slab.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="failure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Each One Fails
          </h2>

          <p>
            Acrylics fail visibly and progressively. UV breaks down the film, traffic abrades it, and moisture that gets underneath lifts it. The sequence is usually dulling, then patchiness, then flaking at the edges and in the traffic lane. Two to four years outdoors is normal, and by the end of it the slab looks worse than an unsealed one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Penetrating sealers fail invisibly. The repellent lining in the pores gradually breaks down, the slab stops beading water, and nothing about the appearance changes to tell you. There is no flaking, no peeling and no cloudiness — just a driveway that is quietly unprotected. Five to ten years is typical, and the only way to know where you are is to test with water.
          </p>

          <p>
            The difference in failure mode drives the maintenance. An acrylic needs recoating on a schedule you can see, and eventually stripping once build-up traps moisture. A penetrating sealer needs a water test each spring from about year four, and a refresher coat over a clean slab when the test fails. Our guide to <a href='/how-long-does-concrete-sealer-last/'>how long concrete sealer lasts</a> sets out the schedule for both.
          </p>

          <p>
            Acrylics also fail catastrophically in one specific circumstance: moisture pushing up from below. A slab on grade without a vapour barrier, or one with a high water table beneath it, will push vapour into the underside of the film until it blisters and turns white. A penetrating sealer is immune because it leaves the pores open to vapour, and that single property decides a great many basement and garage floors. Our notes on <a href='/why-is-my-concrete-sealer-turning-white/'>concrete sealer turning white</a> cover the diagnosis.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="freeze" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Freeze-Thaw and De-Icing Salt
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is where penetrating sealers earn their price. Freeze-thaw damage happens when water inside the concrete's pore structure freezes and expands, breaking the paste apart from within. A film sealer stops water entering from above while it is intact, but the moment it wears through in the traffic lane, that area is unprotected — and the traffic lane is exactly where the salt lands.
          </p>

          <p>
            A penetrating sealer lines the pores throughout the depth it reached, typically an eighth to a quarter of an inch. Surface wear does not remove it, because there is nothing at the surface to remove. A driveway that has been ground by three winters of plough blades still has its penetrating sealer working, which is the practical reason it is the default recommendation for northern climates.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Where Each One Stands Up</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Penetrating</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Freeze-thaw driveways</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Penetrating</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Heavy de-icing salt</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Acrylic</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Stamped and coloured</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Acrylic</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Oil and stain resistance</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            On salt specifically, both chemistries reduce chloride ingress, but they do it for different lengths of time. Chloride is the ion that corrodes reinforcement and drives scaling, and it keeps arriving every winter for the life of the driveway. A protection strategy that lapses every third spring is not really a strategy, which is why penetrating sealers dominate on anything that gets salted.
          </p>

          <p>
            The exception is a covered or sheltered surface. A patio under a roof, a garage floor that never sees a plough, or a courtyard in a mild climate faces none of this, and the acrylic's weaknesses never get tested. There, the decision reverts to appearance and stain resistance, where the acrylic is genuinely stronger.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="slip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Slip Resistance, Heat and Tyres
          </h2>

          <p>
            A glossy acrylic on a wet surface is slippery, and on steps, pool surrounds and any sloped walkway that is a real safety issue rather than a theoretical one. The fix is a slip additive — fine polymer grit or aluminium oxide broadcast into the final coat — and it should be treated as standard rather than optional on anything anyone walks on when wet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Penetrating sealers do not change the surface profile at all, so slip resistance is exactly what the concrete finish provides. On a broom-finished driveway that is perfectly adequate. This is one of the quiet advantages of penetrating chemistry around pools and steps, and the reason it dominates in our recommendations for <a href='/best-pool-deck-sealer/'>pool deck sealers</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heat is the other differentiator. A car parked on a hot acrylic film with hot tyres can soften the film and pull it away when the tyres cool and grip — the same hot tyre pickup that afflicts poorly installed garage epoxy. Solvent-based acrylics are more vulnerable than water-based ones, and a dark slab in full sun is the high-risk case.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is no equivalent failure for a penetrating sealer, because there is no film for a tyre to grab. If your driveway is in full sun and cars sit on it all afternoon in summer, that alone is a reasonable basis for choosing penetrating chemistry regardless of anything else in this comparison.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="surfaces" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Matching the Sealer to the Surface
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most of the confusion in this decision disappears once you state what the surface is and what it has to survive. The table below is the recommendation we would give without knowing anything else about the job.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Surface</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Recommended</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete driveway, cold climate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Penetrating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Salt and freeze-thaw, heavy traffic wear</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete driveway, mild climate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Either</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Acrylic acceptable if appearance matters</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Stamped or coloured patio</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Acrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Colour and sheen are the objective</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Exposed aggregate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Acrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Binds surface stones, enhances colour</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pool deck</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Penetrating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slip resistance and constant wetting</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Garage floor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Epoxy or polyaspartic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Abrasion and chemical resistance</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Basement floor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Penetrating or densifier</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Vapour must be able to escape</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Walkway and steps</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Penetrating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slip safety when wet</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Garage floors are worth calling out because neither column of this comparison really applies. A garage floor takes hot tyres, dropped tools, oil and solvent, and what it wants is a thick, bonded coating system rather than a sealer — which is why the options there are epoxy and polyaspartic, covered in our <a href='/best-garage-floor-epoxy/'>garage floor epoxy guide</a> rather than here.
          </p>

          <p>
            Basement floors are the other special case. Anything film-forming over a slab with moisture beneath it is a blistering risk, and basement slabs frequently have no vapour barrier at all. Penetrating sealers and silicate densifiers are the safe choices, for the same vapour-permeability reason set out in our <a href='/basement-floor-sealer/'>basement floor sealer</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="switching" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Switching From One to the Other
          </h2>

          <p>
            Going from acrylic to penetrating requires removing the film completely. A penetrating sealer cannot reach the pores through a layer of acrylic — it will sit on top, stay tacky and never cure properly. Chemical stripper or mechanical grinding, then a thorough wash and a full dry, and only then the new product. It is a real job and it is not optional.
          </p>

          <p>
            Going from penetrating to acrylic is harder, and often impossible in practice. A silane-siloxane has made the pore walls water-repellent, and a water-based acrylic will not bond to that. Solvent-based acrylics stand a slightly better chance but frequently produce patchy adhesion. If you might ever want the wet look, do not start with a penetrating sealer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Recoating like with like is straightforward in both directions. Acrylic over cured acrylic bonds well provided the surface is clean and the existing film is sound. Penetrating over penetrating simply refreshes the pore lining, and does so without any risk of build-up. This is another quiet advantage on the penetrating side: maintenance is always the same job.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do not know what is already on the slab, test before you buy. Water that beads tells you something repellent is present. Acetone on a rag that softens the surface tells you a solvent acrylic is there. If water beads and acetone does nothing, you have a penetrating sealer, and your options are refreshing it or grinding the surface.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing by How Wet the Sample Looks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The darkened, glossy sample board sells acrylic every time, but a driveway that gets salted and ploughed needs pore-depth protection that survives surface wear. Decide what the slab has to survive first, then decide how it should look.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a Film Over a Damp Slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Concrete with moisture moving up through it will blister and whiten an acrylic film from underneath. Basement floors and slabs without vapour barriers need a vapour-permeable penetrating sealer or a densifier, not a film.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Slip Additive</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Glossy acrylic on wet steps, walkways and pool surrounds is genuinely hazardous. Broadcast a slip additive into the final coat on any surface people walk on when it is wet. It costs a few dollars and cannot be retrofitted later.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying Penetrating Sealer Over Existing Acrylic</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It cannot reach the pores through a film, so it sits on the surface, stays tacky and never cures. The old film has to be stripped or ground off completely first — there is no shortcut and no product that works around it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming You Can Change Your Mind Later</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A penetrating sealer is permanent, and acrylic will not bond reliably to a water-repellent surface afterwards. If there is any chance you will want a wet look on that patio, do not start with penetrating chemistry.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is penetrating sealer better than acrylic?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For exterior concrete that faces freeze-thaw, de-icing salt or vehicle traffic, yes — it protects inside the pore structure where surface wear cannot reach it, and lasts five to ten years against two to four. For decorative concrete where colour and sheen are the objective, acrylic is the right product and penetrating sealer does nothing at all visually.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put acrylic sealer over penetrating sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not reliably. The penetrating sealer has made the pore walls water-repellent, and a water-based acrylic will not bond to that surface. Solvent-based acrylics sometimes adhere but often go on patchy. Decide on appearance before you apply any penetrating product, because the decision is effectively permanent.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which sealer is best for a concrete driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In a climate with winter salting and freeze-thaw cycles, a silane-siloxane penetrating sealer. It survives plough and tyre wear because there is no surface film to remove, and it will not peel or blister. In a mild climate where appearance matters more than durability, a solvent acrylic is a reasonable alternative.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does penetrating sealer change the look of concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Applied correctly, no. It should be invisible once cured. If a penetrating sealer has darkened the slab or left a sheen, it was applied too heavily and excess product dried on the surface — which can usually be corrected by scrubbing with the matching solvent while it is fresh.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will acrylic sealer peel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can, and that is its main failure mode. Peeling happens when the film is applied over a dirty or damp slab, applied too thick, or subjected to vapour pushing up from beneath. A well-applied acrylic on a dry, clean, well-drained slab wears away gradually rather than peeling, but it still needs recoating every two to four years.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which lasts longer, acrylic or penetrating sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Penetrating, by a factor of two to four. A silane-siloxane gives five to ten years on a residential driveway; a water-based acrylic gives one to three and a solvent acrylic two to four. The gap widens further in salted, high-traffic conditions where film wear is fastest.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use penetrating sealer on a stamped patio?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, and it will protect the concrete, but it will do nothing for the colour and sheen that stamping is usually done for. Stamped work is one of the few cases where the decorative film is the point, so an acrylic — with a slip additive — is the right choice despite its shorter life.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

